e row lists `cache_control`. The output cap stays the configured limit: the agentic path's `ATLAS_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS` (or the model's completion limit when that is unset) and the one-shot path's `CallLimits` cap. **Options.** Leaving the model and the four slugs hardcoded keeps today's pin and blocks the trial models. Relaxing `require_parameters` would let the request carry `cache_control` and `reasoning.max_tokens` onto endpoints that do not advertise them, which is the refusal this payload rule avoids. Treating Safety Retention as pure ZDR would send, for example, Gemini on Vertex to endpoints that may retain flagged prompts. **Cost.** Each review adds one unauthenticated GET of the ZDR list per provider. A one-slug pin does not fail over to another host; the default pin stays four slugs, so an unset configuration does not take that outage. Sonnet stays the default until a cheaper model passes the parity gate on #643: an extra finding does not fail that gate, and it fails when a selected lens was NOT RUN or failed, a lens named by a reference finding was not run, file coverage is incomplete, or recall of the reference findings is below 1 (that PR's D45 addendum). **Reversibility.** Unset the three variables and drop the flags. The defaults are the previous model and pin, and Safety Retention stays off. Nothing is migrated. A parent slug is refused when any child tag (`<slug>/...`) for that model is Safety Retention or has no boolean `safety_retention`, because `provider.only` of the parent can expand to that child. A slug with no exact row is refused and the error names child tags when the list has them. `max_price` stays the model's highest catalog prompt and completion rates, including overrides, which can be above the eligible rows and so cannot admit a row the reservation did not price. A required prompt or completion rate of zero is refused, and a per-million price that is not finite is refused. The catalog and the ZDR list are read once per provider and reus